s  numpy  and then  we can write functions on top of those  desk arrays do things like say a slope  calculation or a hillshade calculation  but be using a dash array and have that  um actually  resolved across a cluster as opposed to  just having to to rely on one machines  in memory um capacity  and then coupe i mentioned a little bit  about kupai but kupai is the library  you'd use to  have an array on top of a cuda enabled  gpu  our general philosophy in x-ray spatial  is that you give us a xray data array  and we  introspect that array and identify what  type of array is that it's using is it  using numpy dash or coupei and then  we'll return the same object to use so  if you send in a  an xray data array that's backed by a  dash array then that's what you'll get  back from the analysis function so there  isn't any flags to set like hey use a  gpu or not if you send us a coupon  array we'll do our best to use a gpu  that's that's  there um  it would be a little remiss to not  mention um how vector data ties in here  so geopandas is a really great library  for managing  vector data so this would be  the equivalent of adding a geometry  column into a pandas data frame is  essentially what geopandas does but then  has also um  geospecific functions on top of the  geometry so geopandas adds the geoseries  so that we can store points lines and  polygons but many times we need to bring  those vectors into a raster based  analysis  and that's where we can take a geopandas  geodata frame  and feed it through a project called  data shader xray spatial actually fell  out of data shader because i was adding  um a lot of geo functionality to data  shader and finally we looked at looked  at and said you know what let's keep  data shader as just a rasterization  pipeline and all this additional  functionality can move out and that was  the the beginnings of of x-ray spatial  but data shader is a general purpose  rasterization pipeline that will help  you in aligning any of your vector data  with other raster data so inside of data  shader we can take say a bunch of  polygons and we can rasterize them to  the same resolution as say a satellite  image and then be able to do our zonal  operations between the two data sources  so just a  shout out to data shader which is a  really really nice nice project  so when you come to  the x-ray spatial  github page and you install the the  library you can access also  example notebooks this is in the the  user guide area of x-ray spatial some of  them include surface tools we try to  have one for each of the major  categories of of analysis tools  and you can see a little bit about of  how you'd actually execute these tools  so we're starting by generating a a  terrain that's just purlin noise  um we're adding some color mapping and  this is using data shader to do the  actual  color stretch from on a pseudoelevation  color map  but then here we see say an x-ray  spatial tool  and this is  the style of how you call the tools from  x-ray spatial you would import  the tool in this case it's a hill shade  and then you'd pass in an x-ray data  array and then you'd get an x-ray data  right back from there you could shade it  using data shader or you could save it  to disk using x-ray but  this analysis tool is the part that's  really x-ray spatial so there's uh this  these notebooks cover many a different  many different tools  and could certainly spend a lot of time  just on one notebook but i also wanted  to show  proximity tools  so here within proximity tools  this is particularly useful tool set for  feature engineering so if you're trying  to pull out new features for your model  and think that spatial  relationships could be important then  you may take a look at using the  proximity tools to do things like hey  what's the distance at this pixel to the  nearest gas station maybe that could be  an interesting question or dimension to  add into your model so i encourage folks  to not just think okay this is just for  geospatial folks these tools also very  much lend themselves to just general  general ml  here we start with  a bunch of target pixels  these are we can think of as our  locations that we're going to calculate  distance to  and then we run them through  x-ray spatial proximity function and we  generate out a distance map  where each pixel would be  the distance to the nearest target  and there's different flavors of doing  distance analysis so just the raw  distance is one and you can obviously  change a distance metric in this case  we're using a great circle distance but  then there's also distance tools like  and we can obviously threshold things  but allocation so distance allocation  would say  hey i want to generate a grid but i want  the pixel value to be the id of the  nearest feature not the actual raw  distance  or another you know flavor of that would  be i want the bearing or direction to  the nearest target pixel so these are  some of the  proximity functions that we support in  x-ray spatial  then also the zonal tools i just wanted  to show real quick um using zonal tools  in this case um it may be a little  difficult to see but we have a a fake  terrain here and i have a lot some line  features that go across the terrain that  represent a  a hypothetical height  where each um segment is one day of the  height  and we want to calculate some summary  statistics about the terrain for each  day of the height and that's where we  can use zonal stats tools we can feed in  our lines as our  zones and then we have values underneath  which are in the elevation raster that  that's below  those lines and what we get back here  this would be one example of not getting  a x-ray data right back in this case you  get a pandas data frame that's indexed  by the id of each one of the zones and  then has summary stats for each one of  the zones and you can define your own  functions  for how to calculate those summary stats  so here i've written a  a simple function and that will inside  of this function when it's called i'll  only have the pixels that apply for that  zone and i can do more  subtle calculations on just the pixels  of that zone so these are zonal  statistics tools  in a little bit into the code so if you  say hey i'm not that interested in the  spatial stuff but you are interested in  number and desk i would encourage you to  go and dive into the x-ray spatial code  and um slope might be a good one to look  at  here if we scroll down in slope to look  at some of the code here and make it a  little bigger  we can see um  [Music]  the uh so this is horn slope it's the  you know the first derivative of the  elevation  and we can see the cpu implementation of  horn slope which uses number for  speedups  um a little ways down  we can see the gpu implementation  and um as we keep going we can see so  there are some some things that we're  still still working out here this  probably uh could be could be fixed but  we can see the use of dask's  map overlap which is um a shows you on  the on the desk side how you can write a  function which deals with overlapping  partitions on your dash array so if you  need the edges on one partition to do  something like calculated neighborhood  then you can use the map overlaps  function inside of das to do that and  then these are all um coordinated  together in a public  method or public function called slope  that will figure out um what type of  array back-end you're using and then  call the appropriate  slope function based on the array that  you pass in  so right now x-ray spatial is being used  in a couple of places one of them is the  microsoft planetary computer the  planetary computer for folks that don't  know is um is a initiative from  microsoft to combine curated data sets  with um scalable compute and open source  tools so we have a data catalog that  is uh is has tons of different imagery  and then um we can run jupiter hub on  top of it and this is what uh the  microsoft computer looks like if you  were to actually log in so we're logged  in to  to the uh planetary computer right now  we have a bunch of tutorial notebooks  that lead you through different analyses  with x-ray spatial and other tools  and you can run them all  on das clusters  posted on on azure access through  jupyter lab so this is a really nice  tool from microsoft that um you can see  uh x-ray spatial in action  then also we've been having fun recently  just trying to say like what what parts  of x-ray spatial would benefit the most  from better gpu support so we chose um  you know four different areas over the  last couple of weeks to just focus on  better gpu  support and performance um terrain  generation view shed and um and hill  shading  now while a lot of our um the benchmarks  where we were comparing a single core  cpu to a single gpu  have to be taken with a grain of salt  like we can see hey in the example of  generating purlin noise our cpu  implementation was taking 25 seconds on  this 4000 by 2000 pixel grid while uh  the the cuda version was blowing it out  of the water but these have to be taken  with a grain of salt because this is  just a single core cpu so the cpu  implementation could be different and  then also um uh this you know could be  could scale to say if we had 20 cpus  what would that performance be but it  still yields some some very nice results  from um focusing on poopai and uh cuda  on top of with x-ray spatial  um we also did this for view shed to  make a um a cuda enabled view shed  viewshed would be  having an observer at a specific height  and then identifying those pixels within  the grid that are in direct line of  sight from uh from the user  um we did this also with our basic hill  shade which is just a three by three  calculation  of normals around a pixel but then um  also we did a  ray tracing version of hill shade which  allows you to actually put more  realistic shadows on the terrain  um we got 